# snapshotted

Write the application once in `snapshotted_app`. The browser, web SSR server, and native runners render the same screen and counter behavior. `snapshotted_backend` implements RPC endpoints; `snapshotted_shared` holds their serializable contracts. The root server imports the app and backend, while application builds never import backend implementation.

```sh
leptoskit build
leptoskit dev
leptoskit build --platform ios
leptoskit build --platform android
leptoskit build --platform macos
leptoskit build --platform windows
leptoskit build --platform linux
```

All six destinations are enabled in `leptoskit.toml`. Each build activates exactly one platform feature in `snapshotted_app`; add optional dependencies to that feature when they support the app's actual compilation target. Native app code compiles to Wasm, so UIKit, JNI, and other OS dependencies belong in the corresponding runner's Cargo manifest or a native plugin.

`src/main.rs` starts the SSR server and serves browser files. The backend's `health` server function answers `GET /api/health`, and `#[api]` publishes it: `leptoskit rpc spec` prints the published API, and `leptoskit rpc generate --target typescript` writes a typed client for it. Shared assets live in `assets/`. `web/index.html` supplies the document for CSR mode (`app.render = "csr"`); SSR uses the shared app's `shell` function.

## Platform overrides

`snapshotted_app/src/routes.rs` declares the home module once:

```rust
leptoskit::platform_module!(pub mod home = "routes/home");
```

The module path is relative to the app crate's `src/` directory. Add `routes/home.ios.rs`, `routes/home.android.rs`, `routes/home.web.rs`, `routes/home.macos.rs`, `routes/home.windows.rs`, or `routes/home.linux.rs` to replace `routes/home.rs` on that platform. Imports stay `crate::routes::home`. The shared file is the fallback; replacements must expose the same interface. Use this declaration for any module that needs platform overrides. The build helper watches additions and removals of override files.

`ios/`, `android/`, `macos/`, `windows/`, and `linux/` are app-owned platform runners. Install the matching SDK when building a destination. All generated registrants, SDK integration files, and build intermediates stay under `.leptoskit/<platform>/`, which is ignored by Git. Prepare a native target before opening its generated Xcode project or the app-owned `android/` Gradle project.

`leptoskit dev` resolves `monosecret.toml` through Monosecret before starting. Install Monosecret, then store credentials in its keyring or your team's provider. The manifest declares names; keep secret values out of Git. Use `leptoskit dev --no-secrets` when your host supplies the environment. In production, run `monosecret run -- ./target/release/snapshotted`. See [secret management](https://leptoskit.dev/docs/guide/secrets.html).

The starter requests no native plugin capabilities. Add native plugins to the runner's direct Cargo dependencies and grant them explicitly in `native.plugins`.

## Database

`snapshotted_db` declares the tables as Rust structs. The server sees every column; clients only ever get the `Public…` types, which leave out `private` and `secret` columns. Create the first migration, then run the server, which applies pending migrations as it starts:

```sh
leptoskit db migration create init
leptoskit dev
```

While `leptoskit dev` runs, a change to the tables that keeps every row becomes a migration under `migrations/`, applied straight away; a change that would delete data writes nothing and says which command to run. You can also run `leptoskit db migration create <name>` yourself. Review and commit what it writes. `leptoskit db check` fails when the tables changed without a migration, so run it in CI. The server keeps its data in `snapshotted.sqlite3`, in the directory it starts from, unless `DATABASE_URL` names another database.

## Operations

`snapshotted_shared` declares the app's queries and mutations next to its contracts: `notes` lists the newest notes, and `add_note` adds one. Every screen calls them the same way, in the browser, in a native app, and while the server renders a page: `leptoskit::rpc::client::call(AddNote { body }).await`. Their bodies, and the database code in them, compile only into the server, through `snapshotted_shared`'s `server` feature. A native app's calls go to `SERVER` in `snapshotted_app/src/lib.rs` with the signed-in person's token, since the sign-in client's `configure` sets this client up too. Each runs as whoever signed in, and the `Note` table's policy shows and changes only their own notes; before sign-in, both answer `401`.

The server answers each at `POST /api/<name>`, with its arguments as one JSON object: `POST /api/notes` with `{}`, and `POST /api/add_note` with `{"body": "Milk"}`. A failure answers `Kind|message` as text with its status, such as `400` for an empty note. The live socket at `/live` sends a subscribed query's result again whenever a write changes it.

`#[api]` publishes both, with the backend's `health`, so generated clients call the same routes and subscribe over the same socket:

```sh
leptoskit rpc list
leptoskit rpc generate --target typescript --out clients/typescript
```

`leptoskit rpc` builds the server and runs it with `LEPTOSKIT_RPC_SPEC_OUT` set: it writes the published API and exits before it reads its configuration, opens the database, or loads any keys, so it needs neither `DATABASE_URL` nor secrets.

## Sign-in

The app starts on a sign-in screen: an email address, then a code emailed to it. The same two steps create an account for a new address, and each new account gets a row in `snapshotted_db`'s `User` table. Once signed in, the bar at the top shows where you're signed in and signs you out.

The server needs its keys before it starts. Store them with Monosecret; `leptoskit dev` resolves them from the generated manifest:

```sh
monosecret set AUTH_ENCRYPTION_KEYS --provider keyring "$(date +%Y-%m):$(openssl rand 32 | basenc --base64url | tr -d =)"
```

Before you deploy, set `origin` in `[auth]` to your site's URL, such as `https://myapp.com`, and say how to send email in `[auth.mail]`. In a debug build, emails aren't sent: open `/_leptoskit/mail` to read them, or sign in to any address without a code by posting `{ "email": "ada@example.com" }` to `/auth/dev/sign-in`. Release builds have neither, and refuse to start without `[auth.mail]`.

The screens live in `snapshotted_app/src/auth/` and are yours to restyle. They work the same in the browser and in native apps. A native app finds the server at `SERVER` in `snapshotted_app/src/lib.rs`. It reaches it through the `http` plugin and keeps its refresh token with `secure-store`; both are granted in `[native]` in `leptoskit.toml`, and the HTTP plugin may reach the development server. Before you ship, set `SERVER` to your site's origin and put that origin in `[native.plugin-options.http] origins`. The Linux, Windows, and terminal runners can't host plugins yet, so there the screens answer that sign-in isn't set up.
